
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- real conditions
body='<div class="callout warning"><strong>Observed history, stated as history.</strong> Every figure on this page is a published official statistic, or a statistic this site computed from a published series with the arithmetic shown. None of it is a forecast. The scenario bands further down are invented planning assumptions, clearly labelled, and they are not predictions.</div>'
body+=section('Why a straight line is the wrong planning tool',table(['What the record shows','Evidence','Why it changes a decision','Source'],[
 ('Inflation is not a constant','Over the 35 complete years from 1991 to 2025, annual CPI-U change averaged 2.62%, ranged from −0.36% to 8.00%, and exceeded 3% in 10 of those years.','A single 3% cost assumption is reasonable in the middle and badly wrong at both ends. Costs that compound quietly for 24 years magnify that error.','cpiHistory'),
 ('Two recent years broke the pattern','CPI-U rose 4.70% in 2021, 8.00% in 2022 and 4.12% in 2023 as annual averages, after a decade mostly under 2%.','A planner who calibrates on 2010–2020 understates cost growth; one who calibrates on 2021–2023 overstates it. Both are extrapolation errors.','cpiHistory'),
 ('The tax base is capped, prices are not','Proposition 13 limits the annual adjustment to a property’s factored base year value to 2%. Across the 51 published assessment years from 1976-77 to 2026-27 the factor hit the full 2% cap in 40 years, was lower 11 times, and compounded at about 1.81% a year.','The assessed base and the market price diverge over time by design. Modeling them with the same growth rate is a structural error, not a rounding error.','prop13factor'),
 ('Official projections stop long before the horizon','CBO’s February 2026 baseline runs 2026 to 2036 and projects PCE inflation returning to roughly 2% around 2030.','A 2045 or 2050 transfer sits far outside any official projection. Anything shown for those years is a scenario, whoever produces it.','cbo'),
 ('Regional hazard is priced separately','USGS estimated a 72% chance of at least one magnitude 6.7 or greater earthquake somewhere in the Bay region between 2014 and 2043.','Standard homeowners policies generally exclude earthquake damage, so an uninsured loss is a genuine balance-sheet event rather than a premium line.','usgs'),
 ('Borrowing is not cheap','Freddie Mac’s survey averaged 7.11% for a 30-year fixed rate as of 12 November 2026.','Any plan that assumes borrowing against a property to fund a repair or a purchase has to clear that hurdle. No borrowing is modeled here.','pmms'),
 ]))
rows=[]
for y,ch in [(1991,'4.24%'),(1995,'2.81%'),(2000,'3.38%'),(2005,'3.39%'),(2008,'3.84%'),(2009,'−0.36%'),(2010,'1.64%'),(2015,'0.12%'),(2020,'1.23%'),(2021,'4.70%'),(2022,'8.00%'),(2023,'4.12%'),(2024,'2.95%'),(2025,'2.63%')]:
    rows.append((str(y),ch,'Annual average, not seasonally adjusted, computed from the published series.','cpiHistory'))
body+=section('The inflation record, selected years',table(['Year','Annual CPI-U change','Note','Source'],rows)+'<p class="tiny">Annual averages, not seasonally adjusted. Computed from the published series; 2026 is excluded because the year is incomplete. The years shown are chosen for range, not to support a trend claim. The full 35-year series is in the data file.</p>')
body+=section('Two rates that move differently',table(['Quantity','Long-run observed behavior','Official basis','Source'],[
 ('Market price of a home','Not published as a national constant. Regional indexes exist; a single parcel is not an index.','FHFA publishes house-price indexes; they are not a forecast for one house.','hpi'),
 ('Assessed value used for tax','Capped at 2% a year absent a reassessment event, and the assessor enrolls the lower of the factored base or market value.','BOE LTA 2026/002 and the Pub 800-10 information sheet.','prop13'),
 ('Effect on the decision','If market growth outruns the 2% assessed cap, an owner who keeps the protected base pays tax on a shrinking fraction of value. A sale resets that base for the buyer, not for this household.','This is the mechanical reason the live-versus-rent gap widens over time.','prop13cap'),
 ('Effect of a reassessment event','A transfer that does not qualify for relief resets the base to market value, which can multiply the tax bill in one year.','Prop 19 relief has occupancy, timing and filing conditions.','prop19'),
 ]))
body+=section('Scenario bands used on this site','<p>These five rates are planning assumptions. Three of them are invented bands; the 2% rate is the official statutory cap, used here as a scenario rather than as a price forecast. The table is generated from the data file so the rates cannot drift from the numbers used elsewhere.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Band</th><th scope="col">Assumed annual inflation</th><th scope="col">Price level after 24 years</th><th scope="col">Price level after 50 years</th><th scope="col">Status</th><th scope="col">Context only</th></tr></thead><tbody id="band-table"></tbody></table></div>')
body+=section('What compounding actually does to a cost','<p>The same four rates, applied to a single dollar of cost. The right-hand column is the part most planning documents omit: a nominal balance is not purchasing power.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Annual inflation</th><th scope="col">A $1 cost after 24 years</th><th scope="col">A $1 cost after 50 years</th><th scope="col">Purchasing power of $1,000,000 after 24 years</th><th scope="col">Context only</th></tr></thead><tbody id="compound-table"></tbody></table></div>')
body+=section('The observation that most affects the ranking','<p>In the invented example on the comparison page, the rental strategy is <strong>cash-negative before income tax</strong> at the assumed growth rate. The property tax line alone, computed on a reassessed market value, is larger than the modeled operating margin. That is not a quirk of the arithmetic; it is what full reassessment does to a long-held California property at today’s price-to-rent ratio.</p><p>The table below finds the gross monthly rent the rental would need just to cover operating costs, before financing and income tax, expressed in today’s money so it can be compared with an actual rent roll. If the achievable rent is below that line, renting loses to selling for reasons that have nothing to do with investment returns.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Quantity, first full year after transfer</th><th scope="col">Invented example</th><th scope="col">What it means</th><th scope="col">Source</th></tr></thead><tbody id="breakeven-table"></tbody></table></div><p class="tiny">All values are from the invented teaching example and are rounded to $10. They are not a rent estimate for any property. The comparison is the point: the rent the model assumes against the rent the model’s own cost structure requires.</p>')
body+=section('Where the operating cost goes','<p>Shares below are computed from the invented example for its first full year after transfer and rounded to a whole percent, so they will not sum exactly to 100. The shape is what matters: property tax and upkeep dominate, and utilities are a minor line for this property type.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Component</th><th scope="col">Live strategy</th><th scope="col">Rent strategy</th><th scope="col">Rule or source</th></tr></thead><tbody id="cost-table"></tbody></table></div>')
body+=section('What this page cannot tell you',table(['Limit','Why','What would resolve it','Source'],[
 ('Whether prices or rents will rise','No official 25-year projection exists, and none is claimed here.','Local comparables and a documented investment policy, reviewed periodically.','cbo'),
 ('What this household would actually pay','The inputs are invented. A real bill depends on the parcel, the rate area and the reassessment outcome.','The county tax bill and an appraisal, kept private.','prop13'),
 ('Whether insurance is obtainable','Availability and price in an earthquake and wildfire state are not determined by a rate filing alone.','Binding quotes, and an explicit decision about earthquake coverage.','cdieq'),
 ('What the trust actually requires','A trust is a private document.','Counsel’s reading of the signed instrument.','trustbasis'),
 ('What future law will be','Bills in this space move every session.','Recheck the watchlist before acting.','hr1301'),
 ]))
page('conditions','A straight line is not a forecast.','The observed inflation and assessment record, the official projection horizon, and the scenario bands this site uses, kept separate from each other.',body,['guide/data/conditions.js','guide/data/model.js','guide/data/breakeven.js','guide/data/conditions-page.js'])
